In Unity3D, if you want a GameObject to not receive touch events, you can achieve this by disabling its collider or by using a script to filter out touch events. Below are a couple of methods to do this:

### Method 1: Disable the Collider
If the GameObject has a collider component (like a BoxCollider, SphereCollider, etc.), you can simply disable it. This will prevent the GameObject from receiving touch events.

```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class DisableCollider : MonoBehaviour
{
    void Start()
    {
        // Disable the collider component
        Collider collider = GetComponent<Collider>();
        if (collider != null)
        {
            collider.enabled = false;
        }
    }
}
```

### Method 2: Filter Touch Events in a Script
If you want more control over which touch events are ignored, you can use a script to filter them out. This is useful if you want to ignore touch events under certain conditions.

```csharp
using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.EventSystems;

public class IgnoreTouchEvents : MonoBehaviour, IPointerDownHandler, IPointerUpHandler
{
    public bool ignoreTouch = true;

    public void OnPointerDown(PointerEventData eventData)
    {
        if (ignoreTouch)
        {
            // Ignore the touch event
            return;
        }

        // Handle the touch event
        Debug.Log("Touch Down");
    }

    public void OnPointerUp(PointerEventData eventData)
    {
        if (ignoreTouch)
        {
            // Ignore the touch event
            return;
        }

        // Handle the touch event
        Debug.Log("Touch Up");
    }
}
```

### Method 3: Use a Canvas Group
If the GameObject is a UI element, you can use a `CanvasGroup` component to block raycasts, which effectively prevents it from receiving touch events.

```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class DisableTouchOnUI : MonoBehaviour
{
    void Start()
    {
        CanvasGroup canvasGroup = GetComponent<CanvasGroup>();
        if (canvasGroup != null)
        {
            // Disable raycast targeting
            canvasGroup.blocksRaycasts = false;
        }
    }
}
```

### Method 4: Use a Layer
You can also use layers to control which GameObjects receive touch events. By setting the GameObject to a layer that is ignored by the touch input system, you can prevent it from receiving touch events.

1. Create a new layer in the Unity Editor (e.g., `IgnoreTouchLayer`).
2. Assign the GameObject to this layer.
3. In your input handling script, check the layer of the touched GameObject and ignore it if it's on the `IgnoreTouchLayer`.

```csharp
using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.EventSystems;

public class LayerBasedTouchFilter : MonoBehaviour, IPointerDownHandler, IPointerUpHandler
{
    public LayerMask ignoreLayer;

    public void OnPointerDown(PointerEventData eventData)
    {
        if (ignoreLayer == (ignoreLayer | (1 << gameObject.layer)))
        {
            // Ignore the touch event
            return;
        }

        // Handle the touch event
        Debug.Log("Touch Down");
    }

    public void OnPointerUp(PointerEventData eventData)
    {
        if (ignoreLayer == (ignoreLayer | (1 << gameObject.layer)))
        {
            // Ignore the touch event
            return;
        }

        // Handle the touch event
        Debug.Log("Touch Up");
    }
}
```

Choose the method that best fits your needs based on the specific requirements of your project.

